data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 11, the Cosmos DB provider **removes sync-over-async support entirely**. The opt-in flag (`EnableSyncOverAsync` or the `CosmosOptionsExtension` workaround) no longer exists. All synchronous LINQ operators — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`, etc. — throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

## Why

The Cosmos DB SDK is async-only. Previously EF Core wrapped calls with `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()`, risking deadlocks and thread-pool starvation. .NET 11 eliminates this entirely.

## The Fix

Convert to async equivalents:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return `Task<T>` / `Task`
- Use `ToListAsync()`, `FirstAsync()`, `CountAsync()`, `SaveChangesAsync()`
- Propagate `async/await` up the call chain (controllers, services, etc.)

If you have callers that absolutely cannot be made async (e.g., legacy interfaces), you'll need to restructure those layers — there is no supported workaround in .NET 11.
